  "preamble": "The complete elision of the velar stop [k] is the final stage of a lenition chain that typically moves from stop to fricative to glide before disappearing entirely. Initial *k was dropped in several Australian Pama-Nyungan subgroups (Northern Paman Uradhi ami < *kami 'mother's mother', Arandic Arrernte ampe- < *ka:mpa-, Kalkatungu, Nhanda), part of the areal phenomenon of initial dropping.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Elision, Lenition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "English",
      "languageFamily": "Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old English cniht 'boy, servant'",
          "to": "knight /naɪt/",
          "note": "Word-initial [k] before [n] was still pronounced in the 16th century and was lost in the 17th; the spelling keeps ⟨k⟩."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old English cnāwan 'to know'",
          "to": "know /nəʊ/"
        },
        {
          "from": "Old English cnēow 'knee'",
          "to": "knee /niː/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Sanskrit to Maharashtri Prakrit",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
